Deliverable is in place: `/app/solution.sparql`.

## What the query does

| Criterion | How it is expressed |
|---|---|
| Full professor | role is a professor title but not "assistant"/"associate" (`?role` + two `CONTAINS` filters) — excludes Athanasios Voulodimos and Stefan Mueller |
| Works in an EU university department | `worksIn → belongsTo → locatedInCountry` with `?euCountry IN (...)` listing the 27 EU member states as of 2025-08-16 |
| A department with >10 current students | sub-`SELECT` per department: students enrolled in courses `isTaughtIn` that department, excluding any whose `hasGraduationDate <= 2025-08-16`, `GROUP BY ?largeDepartment HAVING (COUNT(DISTINCT ?student) > 10)` |
| `?countries` | second, unrestricted `worksIn → belongsTo → locatedInCountry` path, aggregated with `GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT ?country; separator=", ")` |

Using two separate department variables matters: the EU check and the student-count check are independent existential conditions, while `?countries` deliberately covers every department the professor works in (so e.g. Menis gets both GR and US).

## Verification

No SPARQL engine or package manager is installed and the sandbox has no network (`network = false` refused the download), so I validated in two independent ways:

1. A direct Python computation over the parsed Turtle triples (raw triple walk, no query semantics involved) → department counts `ComputerScience_NTUA 12, Engineering_Berkeley 11, HistoryAndPhilosophy_NKUA 12`, and matching professors Tympas, Menis, Attanasio.
2. A strict mini SPARQL 1.1 evaluator I wrote that parses and executes the actual `solution.sparql` file (raising on anything outside the subset, so nothing is silently skipped) → same three rows: `Orfeas Menis (GR, US)`, `Pepe Attanasio (GR, PT)`, `Aristotle Tympas (GR)`.

Both checks agreed. Two data details that the filtering depends on: students with graduation dates in June/July 2025 are no longer current (this is what excludes Marie Dubois, whose Sorbonne department has 15 enrolments but only 6 current students), and `Student5`'s `uni:Bio10` is a typo that matches no course, so it correctly contributes nothing. The scratch checker and the temporary npm install were removed; only `solution.sparql` was added.

One caveat worth stating plainly: the query has never been run through a production SPARQL 1.1 engine, since none was installable here. It uses only standard constructs (sub-`SELECT`, `HAVING`, `FILTER NOT EXISTS`, `IN`, `GROUP_CONCAT`/`DISTINCT`), so it should behave identically under one.

